//! The page's prose, for a reader with new eyes. `[shown|id]` marks a
//! word that opens its plain explanation (docs/glossary.md); `**words**`
//! is bold. A test checks every mark names an idea and no sentence
//! passes a verdict. The numbers quoted are the run's, which is
//! deterministic; the tables beside them are read from the run itself.

pub const LEDE: &str = "The same points kept two ways, as they are in a flat space and as directions on the unit sphere, on two small data sets built with a known structure: three distances between the same pairs, a shortest path and a mean in each space, nearest neighbors and the decision boundary under each distance, a small classifier trained on each representation with everything else held equal, and how badly each representation keeps the data's own distances. The first test of H1.";

pub const HINT: &str = "New to this? A word with a dotted underline opens a plain explanation when you click it (or press Enter on it); click again to close it. The pictures of the sphere turn when you drag them, press the arrow keys once they have the focus, or swipe. The buttons choose which representation a picture shows; turning and choosing change no number.";

pub const QUESTION: [&str; 3] = [
    "Does it matter what shape of space a model keeps its points in?",
    "A model keeps each thing it sees as a list of numbers, a point. Those numbers can be read as a position in a flat room, where the distance between two points is the straight line, or as a direction, a point on the skin of a ball, where the distance is the angle. The two readings put the same numbers in different spaces, and a method that works from distances, nearest neighbors first of all, gives different answers under each.",
    "The worry behind the question is that the choice is usually made by habit: the straight line because it is the default, the angle because word vectors are compared that way. This page makes the choice on purpose, on data whose structure is known, and measures what each choice costs and gains.",
];

pub const BACKGROUND: [&str; 5] = [
    "A **[representation|representation]** is the way a point is kept: the list of numbers, and the space those numbers are read in. Here each point is kept two ways. Flat: as it is, in a room of two or three coordinates, with the straight line as its distance. Spherical: as its direction only, the point scaled to length 1 so that it lies on the unit sphere, with the angle between two points, the [great-circle distance|great-circle], as its distance. Both have the same [ambient dimension|ambient-dimension]; the sphere has one less [intrinsic dimension|intrinsic-dimension], since every length on it is 1. (In the plane the unit sphere is the unit circle; the page says sphere for both.)",
    "Two data sets of the experiment kit, the same ones the baseline page ran, each with a known structure. **Flat clusters**: three clusters of 40 points in the plane, scattered about centers a few units apart (the spread, 1.6, is how far a point is scattered from its center); what tells the clusters apart is where they are, so the straight line is the distance they were built for. **Directions**: four classes of 40 points in three dimensions that differ by which way they point and not by how far out they sit; each point is its class's direction pushed by 0.6 of a random vector (the cone), so two points of one class can be far apart in angle and the classes overlap at their edges; their lengths, from 0.5 to 2, were drawn at random and carry nothing. The angle is the distance they were built for. Angles here are in radians: a quarter turn is pi / 2, about 1.57; a half turn pi, about 3.14.",
    "**[Nearest neighbors|nearest-neighbors]** keeps the training points and answers for a new point with the label most of its k nearest training points have, under whichever distance is chosen; it learns no number. A **[held-out|held-out]** quarter of each set, chosen by a seed, is set aside before anything is trained and the share of it labeled right is the accuracy. The **[decision boundary|decision-boundary]** of a classifier is where its answer changes, drawn by asking it at every point of a grid.",
    "The **classifier** of the training run is a small network: the point's coordinates are multiplied by a matrix of weights (with one extra row, the bias, added to every input), each of the 8 results is squashed into -1 to 1 by the tanh function (a hidden unit), and a second matrix turns those 8 numbers into one score per class, which softmax turns into probabilities that sum to 1. It is trained by lowering the **[cross-entropy loss|cross-entropy]** on the training rows: the gradient of the loss by every weight is found by backpropagation (the chain rule applied layer by layer, from the output back), and Adam steps each weight against its gradient by a learning rate, 0.02, that sets how far each step goes. The same starting weights, steps and rate on each representation of a set. The record's operations column counts multiply-adds, as the baseline page's does: one distance per held-out point per training point for nearest neighbors; two per trained number per training row per step for the network.",
    "**[Distortion|distortion]** measures how badly a representation keeps a set of distances: over every pair of points, the fraction by which the representation's distance is too long or too short, averaged and at worst. Here the distances to keep are each set's own, the structure it was built with: the straight line in the plane for the clusters, the angle for the directions.",
];

pub const RATIONALE: [&str; 2] = [
    "Hold everything equal but the space. The same points, the same split into training and held-out rows, the same seeds, the same k for nearest neighbors, the same network, starting weights, step count and learning rate for the classifier; then read each point flat and on the sphere, and measure. Whatever moves between the two columns is the geometry's doing, because nothing else was allowed to move.",
    "The two data sets were built so that each space has a set that matches it and one that does not: the flat clusters are made of positions, the directions of angles. H1, the [hypothesis|hypothesis] of this page, says the matching space does at least as well and the crossed space costs something; what would count against it, written before the run: the sphere scoring as high as the plane on the clusters, or the plane as high as the sphere on the directions. A [null result|null-result] on either set is reported as one.",
];

pub const PURPOSE: [&str; 2] = [
    "This page is the first comparison of H1: two geometries by two data sets, with the baseline page's nearest-neighbor rows as the row every number here is set against (0.900 on the clusters; 0.750 on the directions, 0.775 with their lengths dropped), and an actual training run, small, on each representation. Every point drawn, every distance, path, mean, boundary cell, loss and accuracy comes from the X_eTaL program at the bottom, run in your browser when the page opened. The pictures are marked [measured or analytical|measured-analytical]; all of them here are measured.",
    "It is not a proof that one space is right: two small sets, one run per cell (one split, one set of starting weights), so no spread over seeds is measured. It is not the hyperbolic half, which fits the tree in three spaces and is the next page. And the classifier is not a trained model of anything outside this page; it is the smallest run that can show convergence and held-out accuracy under each representation.",
];

pub const INTUITION: [&str; 2] = [
    "A sheet of paper and a globe. On the sheet, the distance between two cities is the ruler's; two cities on the same street and two across town are told apart by how far the ruler stretches. On the globe, every city is a direction from the center, and the distance is how far round you must turn to look from one to the other; a city twice as far from the center in the same direction is the same city. Keep positions on the sheet and the globe throws away what tells them apart. Keep directions on the globe and the sheet's ruler adds in the lengths, which say nothing.",
    "The ambient dimension is the room's: three numbers per point in both pictures of the directions. The intrinsic dimension is the space's own: three for the room, two for the sphere's skin. Putting a point on the sphere spends one dimension, the length, on purpose. On data whose length carries nothing that dimension was noise; on data whose position carries everything it was signal.",
];

pub const MATHEMATICS: [&str; 5] = [
    "**Three distances.** For two points p and q with coordinates p_i and q_i: the straight line is sqrt(sum of (p_i - q_i)^2). Their dot product is sum of p_i q_i, and their lengths |p| and |q| are the straight line from the origin to each. The cosine of the angle between them is p . q / (|p| |q|); the [cosine distance|cosine-distance] is 1 minus it; the angle itself, the great-circle distance between the two directions, is acos of it, in radians, 0 to pi. The program finds the angle another way (2 atan2 of |u - v| and |u + v| for the unit points u and v, which loses no digits near 0 or pi) and checks the two agree.",
    "**Shortest paths.** In the flat space the shortest path from p to q is the segment, p + t (q - p) for t from 0 to 1. On the sphere it is the arc of the great circle through the two unit points, which the program draws as 13 points along it; the chord between the same two points, straight through the inside, is 2 sin(angle / 2), shorter than the arc, which is the angle.",
    "**Means.** The plain mean of n points is their coordinate-by-coordinate average; for points on the sphere it falls inside the sphere, since averaging directions shortens them. The [mean on the sphere|frechet-mean] is the point of the sphere whose summed squared distances along the sphere to the points is least, found by stepping along the average of the arrows from the current guess to the points until the arrows cancel.",
    "**Nearest neighbors under the angle.** For unit points, the chord between two is 2 sin(angle / 2), which only grows with the angle, so the straight line between unit points and the angle order every point's neighbors the same way; the program checks this on all 160 unit points. So nearest neighbors under the angle is nearest neighbors by the straight line on the unit points, and that is how it is run. The cosine distance orders them the same way too.",
    "**The classifier and its loss.** With x a point's coordinates, W1 a 3 by 8 (or 4 by 8) matrix whose last row is a bias and W2 a 9 by 3 (or 9 by 4) one: h = tanh(x W1), the hidden layer; z = h W2; the probabilities are softmax(z), each exp(z_c) over their sum. The loss is the cross-entropy, minus the mean over training rows of the log of the true class's probability. The gradient of the loss by W1 and W2 is written out by the Net library (backpropagation) and a test checks it against central differences: (loss(w + h) - loss(w - h)) / 2h at five weights, h = 0.00001. Adam steps each weight by the learning rate times a running average of its gradient divided by the square root of a running average of its square.",
];

pub const EXPERIMENT: [&str; 3] = [
    "Two sets by two representations, four cells; on each cell two methods, nearest neighbors (k = 5) and the network. The flat clusters: seed 1, 3 clusters of 40, spread 1.6, in the plane. The directions: seed 5, 4 classes of 40, cone 0.6, lengths 0.5 to 2, in three dimensions. The split by seed 11: a quarter held out (30 of the clusters, 40 of the directions), the rest trained on. The network: one hidden layer of 8 tanh units then softmax (51 numbers on the clusters, 68 on the directions), the same seeded starting weights for both representations of a set, Adam at rate 0.02 for 300 steps.",
    "The measures: held-out accuracy for both methods; the training loss by step, the loss at the end and the steps to a loss under 0.5 for the network; the distortion of each representation against its set's own distances, after one scale is fitted (an angle and a length are in different units); and on the way there, the three distances on three pairs, a path and a mean in each space, and the decision boundary of nearest neighbors on a grid.",
    "Written before the run. On the clusters the sphere should cost accuracy, since it throws the positions away and keeps only which way from the origin each point lies: clusters that lie in the same direction at different distances merge. On the directions the sphere should gain a little, as the baseline page's lengths-dropped row already did, and the network may gain less, since a network can learn to ignore the lengths on its own. The distortion of the crossed cells should be large at their worst pair: two points in nearly the same direction at different lengths are far in the flat space and near on the sphere.",
];

pub const RUN: [&str; 2] = [
    "The run is the program: nearest neighbors on each held-out quarter under each distance, and four training runs of the network, one per cell, each 300 steps from the same weights, the training loss kept at every step. Nothing is tuned to the result: the step count, rate and seeds were fixed before the run and are the same for both representations of a set.",
    "The loss curves below are the four runs' training losses by step; the convergence table reads off each curve's first and last values and the first step under 0.5. The held-out accuracies in the record are the network's at the last step, on the same held-out rows nearest neighbors was scored on.",
];

pub const ANALYSIS: [&str; 3] = [
    "The record, one row per run, every cell measured. Nearest neighbors: on the flat clusters the sphere costs, 0.90 to 0.77, four more of the 30 held-out points wrong, because the three clusters lie in two directions from the origin and two of them merge on the circle. On the directions the sphere gains one point of 40, 0.75 to 0.775, the baseline's lengths-dropped row again. The network: on the clusters it loses the same way, 0.87 to 0.77; on the directions it comes out the same under both, 0.75, ten of 40 wrong each way, the flat run reaching a training loss of 0.5 at step 203 and the spherical run not reaching it in 300.",
    "What caused each difference. On the clusters it is the lost dimension: a cluster's position is its signal and the sphere keeps only its direction; the boundary picture under the angle shows two clusters sharing a wedge of rays. On the directions, for nearest neighbors, it is the lengths: under the straight line a point far out is far from everything, and a short point is near the short points of other classes; the angle ignores that, and the map's boundary shows it, the straight line's moving with the length and the angle's staying put. For the network the page measures only the outcome: the same held-out accuracy, and a lower training loss under the flat representation. A network with a hidden layer can represent a rule that ignores the lengths, which would be consistent with the tie, but whether this one did is not measured here; nor is why the flat run's loss fell faster. Both are open.",
    "Against H1, by the rule written before the run. The clusters count for it: the matching space scored higher and the crossed one cost, under both methods. The directions under nearest neighbors count for it by one point of 40, which is within what one point can move. The directions under the network are a tie, and the rule said the plane scoring as high as the sphere counts against: so that cell counts against H1, a [null result|null-result] reported as one. The distortion rows say what the crossed spaces lose in distance terms, 0.49 on average for the clusters on the sphere and 0.37 for the directions kept flat, with the worst pair of the directions off by a factor of 79: two points in nearly the same direction at lengths far apart, whose scaled straight-line distance is 79 times their angle.",
];

pub const EXTENSION: [&str; 2] = [
    "What this rests on: the cosine comparison of word vectors and the unit-sphere embeddings of face and sentence models, which drop the length on purpose; and the measure of distortion, used by Nickel and Kiela (2017) and Gu, Sala, Gunel and Re (2019) to compare the spaces a set of distances is embedded in, read at source (docs/papers.md). What remains open here: the spread over seeds (one split, one starting weight set), a network given the length as an extra input beside the direction against one given the direction alone, and the directions with the lengths made to carry a class, where the sphere would lose.",
    "What this page does not establish: that any real data set is directional or flat. The two sets were built to be; a real one's structure is measured, by this page's distortion against a candidate space, not assumed.",
];

